WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.966 --> 00:00:01.501
WIL WILLIS: Theo.

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.268
Joe.

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:03.335
Congratulations.

00:00:03.435 --> 00:00:05.037
The strength of your
blades has earned you

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:08.074
a spot in the final
round of our competition.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.309
When you came here, you
used our forge and our tools

00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:12.044
and our equipment
to forge signature

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:13.379
blades in your own style.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.514
Now we're sending you
back to your home forges,

00:00:15.614 --> 00:00:18.884
where you will recreate an
iconic blade from history.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:20.920
Are you ready to see
what that weapon is?

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:21.687
 Absolutely.

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:22.755
 Yes.

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:26.693
 That weapon is the naginata.

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:30.730
 [bleep].

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:32.098
All right.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:33.532
WIL WILLIS (VOICEOVER):
The naginata emerged around

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:35.902
1,000 years ago in Japan
as a powerful pole weapon

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.770
used by samurai warriors.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.840
Featuring a single-edged,
slightly curved blade mounted

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.275
on a wooden shaft, this
7- to 8-foot weapon

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.077
was ideal for
warding off attacks

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:47.046
from enemies on horseback.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.082
Because it relied on
momentum rather than

00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:51.517
upper body strength,
it was an ideal weapon

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:53.119
for female warriors.

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:55.954
The naginata was the weapon
of choice for samurai wives

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:57.323
defending their
homes, and it was

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.493
even wielded by Seong
Mina in the video game

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.261
series "Soulcaliber."

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:04.363
The traditions of
the naginata continue

00:01:04.463 --> 00:01:05.931
to be preserved
through the teaching of

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:09.001
the martial atarashii naginata.

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:10.603
 Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.773 line:14%
We'll see you in five days.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:16.409 line:14%
[intense rock music]

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.179
JOE: Today is day one.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.315
The first step I'm going to
do is draw out our lay design.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:25.118
Process of making this is going
to be outside of my ballpark.

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:27.720
I am not a Japanese swordmaker.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:28.988
I'm an American knifemaker.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:30.957
But we're going to
give it our best shot.

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:33.292
We're going to do
a Damascus billet.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.928
So we're going to let this
saw blade hopefully do

00:01:35.994 --> 00:01:37.229
some wonderful things for me.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:41.034
Doing Damascus is
essentially tougher to do,

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:43.002
just because there's a
lot of failure points.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.937
So then, 300 to 400
layers, that's 300

00:01:45.003 --> 00:01:47.507
to 400 times that I could fail.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:54.313
But we're going to turn that
into, hopefully, a form today.

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:59.953
Going to put it in here, heat
it up, put some flux on it,

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:02.321
and then this thing's
going to be glowing.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.724
I've never made
a Damascus billet

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:07.192
or blade this large before.

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:09.762
Most stuff I make is
about 9 inches long.

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:13.132
So I have a billet that is going
to be drawn to about 40 inches

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:15.902
in overall length,
which is ridiculous.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.670
So yeah, it's going
to be a challenge.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.206 line:14%
[intense rock music]

00:02:23.542 --> 00:02:25.645
THEO: So the very first
thing I have to do

00:02:25.711 --> 00:02:28.013
is start cutting the
sections that I need.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.283
Here, I'm in a forge
I'm comfortable with

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:31.684
and I have more time.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:33.452
This being the first
day, I would really

00:02:33.552 --> 00:02:35.221
love to just go ahead
and start tackling

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.924
forge welding all the pieces
of the blade together.

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.658
I'm going to do a
three-layer laminate.

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:43.262
So here I have my core material,
and then here's my shell

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.564
material on the outside.

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:46.866
The benefit of the
three-layer laminate

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.634
is that the outside
shell absorbs

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:51.871
the shock, whereas
the hard core or edge

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:55.374
material will stay very sharp.

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.244
I definitely have the advantage
over Joe when it comes to this,

00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:01.180
simply because I've made large
blades many times before,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.415
and this will be his first.

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:05.117
Well, I can see
that the outer layer

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:09.088
is starting to bow outwards.

00:03:09.154 --> 00:03:10.423
This is a big piece of metal.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.157
I may have bitten off
more than I could chew.

00:03:12.224 --> 00:03:14.160
It's starting to
look like the layers

00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:15.328
are separating from each other.

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:16.796
They're expanding
at such a great rate

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:20.399
that even some of the
tack welds may be busted.

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.468
This is a problem,
because it means

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.371
I'm going to be constantly
chasing the material to get it

00:03:25.437 --> 00:03:27.773
to re-weld to the other steels.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.309
It's delaminating already.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.310
I have a decision to make.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:33.012
Do I continue to pursue?

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:36.316
It's going to be a headache
if I continue down this path.

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:36.915
No.

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:38.317
I can't.

00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:41.186
Ultimately, it's going
to require too much time.

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:42.888
I'm going to have to
strip off the outer shell

00:03:42.988 --> 00:03:45.024
and just do a pure W2.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.659
It's going to be a
mono steel blade.

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:49.995
I trusted my gut, and
my gut lied to me.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:50.830
[bleep]

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:56.736 line:8%
JOE: Today is the
morning of day two.

00:03:56.836 --> 00:04:00.406
And the plan for today is to go
ahead and draw the blade out,

00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:02.508
grind it, and heat treat it.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.610
So we've got a lot of
work ahead of us today.

00:04:04.676 --> 00:04:08.214
Hopefully everything
goes according to plan.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:09.381
But it's got a diet.

00:04:09.481 --> 00:04:13.085
We want SlimFast or GrindFast.

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:15.321
So I have a blade.

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:16.522
It's pretty square.

00:04:16.588 --> 00:04:18.057
There's a lot of
little finishing to do,

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.793
but it's actually
ready for a heat treat.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.730
I am scared out of my mind.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.799
[intense musical buildup]

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:30.970
[bleep]

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.171
I have to get down.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.372
Trying to move some
of the equipment,

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:34.840
move the propane
tank out of the way,

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.377
because I really don't want
to burn the building down.

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.217
When you change a variable,
you get different results.

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:48.020
I've never worked with
that particular oil before,

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.855
and we'll heat treat again.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:50.757
[bleep]

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:56.328 line:8%
THEO: I have no regrets in
dropping the lamination at all.

00:04:56.428 --> 00:04:59.265 line:8%
Today, I need to create
an integral guard.

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:01.567 line:8%
I'm continuing with a process
that I'm familiar with

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.503 line:8%
and I know I'll
do a good job on.

00:05:06.638 --> 00:05:10.442
I decided to go with just
a simple, low-count twist,

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:12.545
but it'll give it the
additional wrought iron

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.380
feel that I've been going for.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.515
Integral guards are very
European, in general.

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:19.585
But I really think it
will work for this piece.

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:21.020
It looks nice.

00:05:21.086 --> 00:05:23.522
Are guards important on a knife?

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:26.091
Well, they can be integral.

00:05:26.191 --> 00:05:27.393
[drum roll]

00:05:31.363 --> 00:05:32.731
Never done anything
with a handle

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:36.368
this long, so brand
spanking new for me.

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:39.705
Ash is historically a
very nice, flexible wood.

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:41.374
So this will be
great because it'll

00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:43.242
be able to take the shock
of hitting something really

00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.978
hard, like bone or armor.

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:46.746
Oh, yeah.

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:52.752 line:14%
JOE: Morning of day four.

00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:54.386
We're just going
to do a hack test.

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:56.556
It's more than sharp enough
to hack through a tree.

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:00.727
Sweet.

00:06:00.793 --> 00:06:01.861
Worked all right.

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:04.263
The plan for today is
to trim down the half

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.265
and then just fit
everything together

00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:08.300
and work on the finishing.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.237
This is my piece of ash.

00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:12.604
It's supposed to
be vertical grain.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:15.841
It's a little off-center,
but it's not horrible.

00:06:15.941 --> 00:06:19.444
I've never really hafted
a sword to a long pole.

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:21.380
If one little part
of the hafting fails,

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:22.715
then the whole thing fails.

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:24.650
And so there's a lot of
little working parts.

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:28.554
I'm really interested to see
what Theo brings, because I

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.022
feel like I'm going
to give him a really

00:06:30.088 --> 00:06:31.624
good product to compete with.

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:37.463 line:8%
THEO: The heat treat
went very well.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:40.132
Ultimately, it's
about little details.

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:42.101
This is same, manta ray skin.

00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:45.905
It's a traditional wrapping
material for handles.

00:06:45.971 --> 00:06:47.540
That's beautiful.

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:50.209
I have no intention of doing any
testing on the weapon myself,

00:06:50.276 --> 00:06:51.677
because the last
thing I want to do

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:54.346
is push the weapon to
its limit and break it.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:55.915
No, thank you.

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:56.816
This is a damn good blade.

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:58.550
And that's what I
came here to do,

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.687
is to forge a weapon that's
going to knock their socks off.

00:07:05.257 --> 00:07:07.693
DOUG MARCAIDA: The naginata
was a Japanese weapon known for

00:07:07.793 --> 00:07:09.228
its long-range cutting ability.

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:12.064
To see how sharp your naginata
is, I will take your weapon,

00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:14.366
and I'll attempt to cut
through these tatami mats

00:07:14.433 --> 00:07:15.600
and a side of beef.

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:17.135
Theo, you're up first.
You ready?

00:07:17.202 --> 00:07:17.937
 Yeah.

00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:18.871
Yeah, I think so.

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:20.206
Let's do this.

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:21.740
THEO: Oh, jeez.

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:22.841
All right.

00:07:22.908 --> 00:07:23.876
I'm nervous.

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:25.477
The area where the
guard meets the tang

00:07:25.544 --> 00:07:28.046
is a little narrow,
which could result

00:07:28.146 --> 00:07:32.485
in bending, maybe a snapping.

00:07:32.551 --> 00:07:36.722
[suspenseful percussive music]

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:41.961
 I'll take it.

00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:43.129
Yeah, all right.

00:07:43.196 --> 00:07:44.697
DOUG MARCAIDA: I
just want to point

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:46.798
out that, after that test, your
blade has a slight bend to it.

00:07:46.865 --> 00:07:49.801
It almost cut all the way
through one of the tatami mats.

00:07:49.901 --> 00:07:53.906
When it came to the side of
beef, it cut cleanly through.

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:55.074
Your blade feels good.

00:07:55.174 --> 00:07:57.376
It's got a nice weight
to it, a nice balance.

00:07:57.442 --> 00:08:00.446
Overall, your naginata will cut.

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:01.180
Good job.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.348
 Thank you.

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:03.282
 Joe, you're up next.
You ready?

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:04.316
 Oh, yeah.

00:08:04.382 --> 00:08:05.117
DOUG MARCAIDA: Let's do this.

00:08:05.184 --> 00:08:06.685
 Let's do it.

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.854
I feel like Theo did really
good in the tatami mat slice

00:08:08.954 --> 00:08:12.324
and exceptionally well
through the beef slice.

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:13.893
I'm pretty nervous,
at this point,

00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:16.328
on how my blade is
going to perform.

00:08:16.395 --> 00:08:19.499
[intense musical buildup]

00:08:23.335 --> 00:08:24.103
 Nice.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.808
 Oh, well done, man.

00:08:29.875 --> 00:08:32.144
Yeah.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:35.313
 I would say I'm ahead in the
game, performed a little bit

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:36.883
better than what Theo's did.

00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:46.692 line:8%
DOUG MARCAIDA: Well, Joe,
you have a very sharp blade.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:50.196
But we now have an issue.

00:08:50.263 --> 00:08:52.098
When I cut through
the tatami mat,

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:55.768
your handle, at the
seams here, came apart.

00:08:55.834 --> 00:09:00.072
Your handle seam has opened up.

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:03.208
And not only that, it picked up
some cracks on this side that

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:04.676
runs all the way down here.

00:09:04.743 --> 00:09:07.112
And on the other side,
hairline fracture

00:09:07.179 --> 00:09:08.547
that goes down this way.

00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:10.649
The seams are big enough
to where I can actually

00:09:10.715 --> 00:09:13.152
put my fingernail through them.

00:09:13.252 --> 00:09:15.021
That is a critical failure.

00:09:15.121 --> 00:09:17.523
And for purposes of
safety, we cannot

00:09:17.589 --> 00:09:20.125
continue to test your weapon.

00:09:20.225 --> 00:09:20.993
 There's nothing?

00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:22.494
We can't do nothing?

00:09:22.594 --> 00:09:23.896
WIL WILLIS:
Bladesmiths, the judges

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:26.798
have determined that there's
no reason to deliberate.

00:09:26.865 --> 00:09:29.168
Joe, due to the
catastrophic failure

00:09:29.268 --> 00:09:33.172
of your weapon's handle,
you may now leave the arena.

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:41.647
THEO: It's not the
way I wanted to win.

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:43.949
JOE: I wouldn't
say I have regrets.

00:09:44.015 --> 00:09:46.852
I'd say if I could go back and
change some of the things I

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:48.988
did, I would have gone
with a different wood,

00:09:49.054 --> 00:09:51.824
something that was less
likely to open up in a crack.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.893
I feel like I'm in a round room
and I'm looking for a corner

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.095
to cry in.

00:09:56.162 --> 00:09:58.797
It's a pretty tough loss.

00:09:58.864 --> 00:10:00.533
WIL WILLIS: Theo,
congratulations.

00:10:00.633 --> 00:10:01.933
You are the "Forged
in Fire" champion

00:10:02.033 --> 00:10:05.004
and will be receiving
that $10,000 prize.

00:10:05.104 --> 00:10:06.104
Good job.

00:10:06.171 --> 00:10:09.008
[applause]

00:10:09.074 --> 00:10:10.442
 You know what I
like about your blade?

00:10:10.509 --> 00:10:12.077
The way you reinforced
your handle.

00:10:12.177 --> 00:10:15.648
Because the power you
generate with that saved it.

00:10:15.714 --> 00:10:16.548
 Congratulations.

00:10:16.648 --> 00:10:17.950
THEO: Thank you, sir.

00:10:18.017 --> 00:10:20.752
I am elated to be the
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:10:20.819 --> 00:10:24.089
Not the way I wanted to
win, but I'll take it.

00:10:24.156 --> 00:10:27.993
I came here the youngest,
smallest person.

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.127
And I won.

00:10:29.228 --> 00:10:32.031
For the shortest guy, I
certainly came out on top.

